{"approved_definition": "Respect, commitment, and acceptance of the customs and ideas that one's culture or religion provides. Derives from the need for group solidarity: groups develop practices, symbols, ideas, and beliefs that represent their shared experience and fate, which become sanctioned as valued customs and traditions that symbolize the group's solidarity, express its unique worth, and contribute to its survival.", "core_value": "tradition", "sources": [{"entry_id": "02fb94f3:entry:3", "journal_entry": "Can barely keep my eyes open. Sami is teething and it's been three nights of broken sleep. I had to email the client and ask for an extension on the logo revisions. Felt pathetic typing that out. My mother called to say my cousin Rania is getting engaged and when am I bringing Sami to visit. Everything requires something from me and I have nothing left to give right now.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "02fb94f3:entry:2", "journal_entry": "Sami's fever broke. Normal day otherwise. Did laundry, made lentil soup, swept the hallway because nobody else notices the dust. Tariq came home and asked what's for dinner. I said soup. He said his mother already made something. So the soup is in the fridge now, I guess.

Finished a draft of the logo I've been working on. The client wants revisions but at least the base concept is solid. I had to work on it in the bathroom because Sami was asleep in the bedroom and the living room was occupied. Sat on the edge of the bathtub with my laptop balanced on the hamper. It's ridiculous but it's the only place I can close a door without anyone asking what I'm doing.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "02fb94f3:entry:1", "journal_entry": "Sami had a fever all night. I was up every two hours. Tariq slept through it. His mother came in at 5am and took over, told me to rest. I should be grateful but it felt like being dismissed. I'm the one who knows what dose of medicine to give, what temperature means we go to the clinic. I didn't rest.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "02fb94f3:entry:0", "journal_entry": "My mother-in-law rearranged Sami's entire nap schedule without asking me. She told Tariq it was better for the baby to sleep earlier so she could take him to the park in the afternoon. Nobody asked what I thought. I had finally gotten him into a rhythm that gave me two hours in the evening to work on a logo project. That's gone now.

I stood there in the kitchen holding a cup of tea and I just didn't say anything. Tariq looked at me like he expected me to agree. His mother was already moving the crib blanket to the other room. I could feel my jaw clenching but I smiled. I hate that I smiled. Something about the way everyone just decided felt like they forgot I'm his mother, not a guest in this arrangement.", "nudge_response": null}], "user_phrase": "Honoring the customs and practices I was raised with"}
